{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790990256,"nonce":"3jIQeki0YmydOcrPrmBJDd9damI=","message":"Stylesheet proposal, for review before work starts.\n\nGoal: named classes, defined once, that work alongside Tw utilities in class=, checked at compile time like Tw.\n\n1. Declaring rules. A Haxe class marked @:stylesheet, each static a rule whose value is Tw classes, a typed Style, or both:\n     @:stylesheet class Ui {\n       static var card = 'p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface shadow-md hover:shadow-lg';\n       static var title = Style.of({fontSize: 20, fontWeight: 600});\n     }\n   It compiles to the same setters Tw emits, so no CSS-text parser is needed. A .css file read at compile time, as blinc_css does, could come later and lower to the same thing.\n\n2. Using them. class='card p-6' resolves 'card' against the stylesheets in scope (imported, or listed in a compiler define) and 'p-6' against Tw. A name that is neither is a compile error suggesting both kinds.\n\n3. Precedence (to decide). I propose Tailwind's layering: stylesheet rules first (the components layer), then Tw utilities, then style=, then attributes. So 'card p-6' gets p-6's padding whatever the order in class=. The alternative is plain left-to-right order in class=.\n\n4. Variants (to decide). Rules may use hover:, active: and dark: inside their value, and I propose the same prefixes also apply to a rule as a whole (hover:card). Both lower to the existing Variant machinery.\n\n5. Selectors (to decide). Class names only at first: no descendant, element or sibling selectors, so nothing matches at runtime and updates stay fine-grained. A state such as :hover is spelled as a variant, not a selector.\n\n6. Reactivity. A rule's tokens follow the theme as Tw classes do (Themed bindings), and a rule can reference another rule (static var primaryButton = 'button bg-primary').","files":null}]}